  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on macro-level census data from 2005 and 2015, which may not capture more recent shifts in migration dynamics or individual-level decision-making processes.",
      "what": "Using intercity migration data from the 2005 and 2015 Chinese bi-censuses, this study applies an origin-constrained competing destination model and multiple linear regressions to analyze distance sensitivity across different cities. The analysis reveals significant spatial heterogeneity in how distance deters migrants, showing that underdevelopment at the origin restricts migration to shorter distances, while advanced regional development reduces the need for long-distance moves.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the gap in distance sensitivity between different origin cities is narrowing over time, suggesting that local urban development can reduce geographic and administrative inequalities in migration accessibility."
